  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-independent-noise-extends-a-conditional-law": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $X,Y,Z$ be random elements with values in measurable spaces\n$(E,\\mathcal E)$, $(H,\\mathcal H)$, and $(W,\\mathcal W)$. Suppose $Z$ is\nindependent of the random element $(X,Y)$, and let\n$\\kappa:H\\to E$ be a supplied conditional law of $X$ given $Y$. For each\n$h\\in H$, form the product probability\n$\\Lambda(h,\\cdot)=\\kappa(h,\\cdot)\\otimes\\mathcal L(Z)$ on\n$\\mathcal E\\otimes\\mathcal W$. Then $\\Lambda$ is a conditional law of\n$(X,Z)$ given $Y$. If $\\Phi:E\\times W\\to V$ is measurable into a measurable\nspace $(V,\\mathcal V)$, then\n$$\\Lambda^\\Phi(h,C):=\\Lambda(h,\\Phi^{-1}(C))$$\nis a conditional law of $\\Phi(X,Z)$ given $Y$."
    ],
    "alg-kalman-filter": [
      "## Definition\n\nFix a finite horizon $T\\ge0$, positive state dimensions $d_0,\\ldots,d_T$,\nand positive observation dimensions $p_1,\\ldots,p_T$. The input is a\nlinear-Gaussian model from\n[[def-linear-gaussian-state-space-model-with-independent-noises]], with\nmatrices $F_k\\in\\mathbb R^{d_k\\times d_{k-1}}$ and\n$H_k\\in\\mathbb R^{p_k\\times d_k}$, initial mean $m_0$, and symmetric\npositive-semidefinite covariances $P_0,Q_k,R_k$. Its state and observation\nequations are\n$$X_k=F_kX_{k-1}+q_k,\\qquad Y_k=H_kX_k+r_k,$$\nwhere $q_k\\sim N(0,Q_k)$ and $r_k\\sim N(0,R_k)$ are centered and mutually\nindependent across all times and of $X_0$.\n\nThe admissible data are vectors $y_k\\in\\mathbb R^{p_k}$ for which every\ninnovation covariance encountered below is positive definite. Positive\ndefinite means $u^TS_ku>0$ for every nonzero $u\\in\\mathbb R^{p_k}$. The\nalgorithm returns predicted and updated means and covariances and the\ninnovation and gain at each time. Initialize\n$$m_{0|0}=m_0,\\qquad P_{0|0}=P_0.$$\nFor $k=1,\\ldots,T$, perform:\n\n1. Predict:\n$$m_{k|k-1}=F_km_{k-1|k-1},\\qquad P_{k|k-1}=F_kP_{k-1|k-1}F_k^T+Q_k.$$\n2. Form the innovation and its covariance:\n$$v_k=y_k-H_km_{k|k-1},\\qquad S_k=H_kP_{k|k-1}H_k^T+R_k.$$\n3. Factor $S_k=L_kL_k^T$ with lower-triangular $L_k$ having positive\ndiagonal, using\n[[thm-cholesky-factorisation-exists-iff-hermitian-positive-definite-and-is-unique]].\nSolve $L_kU_k=H_kP_{k|k-1}$ and $L_k^TK_k^T=U_k$ by triangular solves. This\ndefines $K_k^T=S_k^{-1}H_kP_{k|k-1}$, hence\n$K_k=P_{k|k-1}H_k^TS_k^{-1}$, without explicitly forming $S_k^{-1}$.\n4. Update:\n$$m_{k|k}=m_{k|k-1}+K_kv_k,\\qquad P_{k|k}=P_{k|k-1}-K_kS_kK_k^T.$$\n\nFor $T=0$ the loop is empty and the returned pair is $(m_0,P_0)$. No\ninverse of $P_0$, $Q_k$, or $R_k$ is used. This item defines the procedure\nand makes no complexity, rounding-error, or numerical-stability claim."
    ],
    "lem-one-step-kalman-gaussian-conditioning": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. Let $U\\in\\mathbb R^d$, $q\\in\\mathbb R^n$, and\n$r\\in\\mathbb R^p$ be mutually independent Gaussian vectors with\n$$U\\sim N_d(m,P),\\qquad q\\sim N_n(0,Q),\\qquad r\\sim N_p(0,R),$$\nwhere $P,Q,R$ are symmetric positive semidefinite. Let\n$F\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times d}$ and $H\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times n}$, and set\n$$X=FU+q,\\qquad Y=HX+r,\\qquad P^-:=FPF^T+Q,\\qquad S:=HP^-H^T+R.$$\nIf $S$ is positive definite, put $K:=P^-H^TS^{-1}$. Then $(X,Y)$ is jointly\nGaussian with mean $(Fm,HFm)$ and covariance\n$$\\begin{pmatrix}P^-&P^-H^T\\\\HP^-&S\\end{pmatrix},$$\nand for every $y\\in\\mathbb R^p$ an everywhere-defined conditional law is\n$$\\mathcal L(X\\mid Y=y)=N_n\\!\\left(Fm+K(y-HFm),\\,P^--KSK^T\\right).$$\nThe conditional covariance is positive semidefinite even if $P$, $Q$, or\n$R$ is singular. It also equals the Joseph expression\n$$ (I-KH)P^-(I-KH)^T+KRK^T.$$\nNo inverse of a state, process-noise, or measurement-noise covariance is\nused."
    ],
    "thm-kalman-filter-is-exact-posterior": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. Fix a finite-horizon model from\n[[def-linear-gaussian-state-space-model-with-independent-noises]] with\nstate and observation equations\n$$X_k=F_kX_{k-1}+q_k,\\qquad Y_k=H_kX_k+r_k\\qquad (1\\le k\\le T),$$\nwhere $X_0\\sim N_{d_0}(m_0,P_0)$, the centered Gaussian noises $q_k,r_k$ are\nmutually independent across times and from $X_0$, and $P_0,Q_k,R_k$ are\nsymmetric positive semidefinite. Let the parameters and observations be\nadmissible for [[alg-kalman-filter]], so the recursively formed innovation\ncovariances\n$$S_k=H_kP_{k|k-1}H_k^T+R_k$$\nare positive definite for every $1\\le k\\le T$. Run that algorithm from\n$m_{0|0}=m_0$ and $P_{0|0}=P_0$. Then, for each $0\\le k\\le T$, the map\n$$\\pi_k(y_{1:k},\\cdot)=N_{d_k}(m_{k|k}(y_{1:k}),P_{k|k})$$\nis an everywhere-defined filtering kernel for $X_k$ given $Y_{1:k}$ in the\nsense of [[def-state-space-filtering-laws]]. The empty history at $k=0$ is a\nsingleton, and $\\pi_0=N_{d_0}(m_0,P_0)$. In particular, these recursions\ngive an exact Gaussian conditional-law version at every observation history;\neach $P_{k|k}$ is positive semidefinite. Singular initial, process,\nmeasurement, predicted-state, and filtered-state covariances are allowed.\nEquivalently, the covariance update can be written in Joseph form\n$$P_{k|k}=(I-K_kH_k)P_{k|k-1}(I-K_kH_k)^T+K_kR_kK_k^T.$$\nOnly the innovation matrices being solved must be positive definite. No\npointwise uniqueness of a conditional-law version on null histories,\nobservation-density assumption, or numerical-stability claim is made."
